# This repository compares exact bytes everywhere: the emitter round-trip
# gate on backlog/knowledge JSON, the golden suites, and the root-vs-template
# parity tests, on every OS. No checkout may convert line endings anywhere,
# so every path is `-text`; the more specific lines below stay as emphasis
# for the especially-critical trees.
* -text

# The frozen fixture corpus (tests/corpus/) is byte-compared verbatim by
# the Rust parity gate (HR-054). `-text` disables Git's line-ending
# conversion on checkin and checkout for these paths regardless of
# core.autocrlf, so a Windows checkout (the CI matrix's windows-latest
# leg) keeps the committed bytes exactly, instead of translating them to
# CRLF and breaking every byte compare.
tests/corpus/** -text

# The reviewed, Rust-generated goldens (tests/goldens/, batch 18 T5) are
# byte-compared the same way the frozen corpus is (render_parity.rs,
# check_parity.rs, read_parity.rs) but were committed without this same
# protection, so the windows-latest CI leg's autocrlf checkout converted
# their LF bytes to CRLF and broke render_parity's byte compare (PR #6,
# run 34056471594). Same fix, same reason: `-text` keeps the committed
# bytes exact on every checkout.
tests/goldens/** -text

# The fixture directories (mini, mini-bad, mini-stale, batch14-workspace,
# invalid-deliverable, skipped-report, stats-workspace) lived under
# tests/corpus/fixtures/ and so carried the first `-text` rule above
# until batch 20 T3 relocated them to tests/fixtures/ (commit e36c4b2)
# without a matching .gitattributes update -- a rename `.gitattributes`
# path rules do not follow. Every check_parity.rs comparison against
# tests/fixtures/mini and tests/fixtures/mini-stale is exact-string
# (check.rs's stale-generated-file compare and its byte/line budget
# checks), so the windows-latest leg's autocrlf checkout re-broke them
# the same way, once cargo test could finally reach that leg (batch 20
# T6, HR-084): check_knowledge_matches_the_frozen_mini_stale_corpus's
# stderr gained a spurious extra stale-file finding and an inflated
# byte count, and check_knowledge_matches_the_frozen_mini_corpus's own
# clean run (no findings expected) picked up a real one from its own
# CRLF-inflated generated files, which flips check-knowledge's stdout
# from "knowledge: ok" to empty (this repository's own mini-bad golden
# shows the same empty-stdout shape for every other "problems found"
# run). Restoring the original tests/corpus/** width at the new path
# fixes both.
tests/fixtures/** -text

# This repository's own generated knowledge surface -- render_all()'s
# entire output set (render.rs: RULE_KINDS's per-area .claude/rules/*.md
# files, standing-rules.md, and the one fixed SKILL_PATH) -- carried no
# `-text` protection at all, unlike the frozen/reviewed test corpora
# above. The consumer is what is new, not the leg: `windows-latest` has
# run `cargo test` in this repository's own ci.yml since 0bb27a0
# (2026-09-04, before batch 20), but dogfood.rs -- and its
# `generated_files_are_fresh_relative_to_knowledge` case, which has no
# JS predecessor -- arrived with batch 20 T1's test port (2174a9f), so
# no Windows run had reached that comparison until batch 20 T6 fix
# round 1 cleared the two gaps above. There, `houserules render --check`
# against this checkout's own live root reported all eight generated
# files "would change" -- the identical CRLF-vs-freshly-rendered-LF
# exact-string-compare mismatch, this time on files this repository
# ships and reads dogfooded, not a test fixture.
.claude/rules/** -text
.claude/skills/project-knowledge/** -text

# The embedded install payload (crates/houserules/src/install.rs) walks
# template/ at compile time and bakes its exact checkout bytes into the
# binary. `-text` keeps every checkout of template/ LF regardless of the
# build host's core.autocrlf, so every build embeds the same bytes and a
# seeded install passes the emitter round-trip gate on every OS.
template/** -text
